<img src="https://assetsio.gnwcdn.com/modern-warfare-4-slide-crop-1.jpg?width=690&amp;quality=85&amp;format=jpg&amp;auto=webp" /> <p>The first session of the Call of Duty: Modern Warfare 4 beta is nearly over <a href="https://www.eurogamer.net/call-of-duty-modern-warfare-4-beta-dates-switch-2-next">after five days of multiplayer action</a>. The beta saw high player numbers <a href="https://www.eurogamer.net/call-of-duty-modern-warfare-4-beta-weekend-one-steam-numbers">on Steam</a> and elsewhere, despite mainly being available to players who pre-ordered the game. Those eager players have had a lot of thoughts to share about their time with Modern Warfare 4 since. And, as you might have expected <a href="https://www.eurogamer.net/call-of-duty-modern-warfare-4-gameplay-debut-community-reactions">given all the pre-beta discourse</a>, gameplay movement has been the main topic discussed, as the faster pace has divided players.</p> <p><a href="https://www.eurogamer.net/call-of-duty-modern-warfare-4-beta-player-reaction">Read more</a></p>

## Summary
The Call of Duty: Modern Warfare 4 beta's first session drew high player numbers on Steam, but gameplay movement has divided the community. Players are complaining about the faster pace of the game and the prevalence of slide-spamming tactics.

## Key Points
- The first beta session of Modern Warfare 4 ran for five days with high player participation, primarily from pre-orderers
- Faster movement mechanics have become the main topic of community discussion
- Slide-spamming and the increased pace of gameplay have divided players positively and negatively
- The beta was largely available to players who pre-ordered the game


